<h2>LSD Radix Sort</h2>
<p>
    Least Significant Digit (LSD) Radix Sort is a sorting algorithm 
    that sorts numbers based on their least significant digit,
    up to their most significant digit.
    <br>
    There are two common variants of LSD Radix Sort:
    <br>
    <br>
    The first variant, which is labeled as <b>'Buckets'</b> in the visualizer settings,
    places elements into 'buckets' based on the value of the current digit.
</p>

<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            For example, if we are sorting the array <b>[1234, 4321, 2134]</b> 
            based on the least significant digit, or ones place,
            we would end up with one bucket for ones, containing <b>4321</b>, 
            and one bucket for fours, containing <b>1234</b> and <b>2134</b>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            After getting all of the buckets, we would write the contents each of each to the array in order, 
            starting with the bucket for 0s, then 1s, and so on.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            This results in the array being <b>[4321, 1234, 2134]</b> after the first pass.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>

            We repeat this process for each digit.

    </li>
</ol>

<p>
    The second variant, which is labeled as <b>'Counting'</b> in the visualizer settings,
    counts how many times each number appears for the current digit.
</p>

<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            If we had the same array <b>[1234, 4321, 2134]</b>,
            after counting the least significant digit, 
            we would see that <b>1</b> appears once and <b>4</b> appears twice.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            We can show this as a second array <b>[1, 2]</b>, 
            with the values representing the occurences of <b>1</b> and <b>4</b> respectively.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Then, we go through the 'occurances' array, 
            setting each value to the sum of itself and the previous value.
            This turns the 'occurances' array into <b>[1, 3]</b>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Then, we create an empty array <b>[-, -, -]</b> and loop backwards through the main array. 
            For each element, we place it at the value listed in the ocurrances array for its current digit.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            The first element we find is <b>2134</b>. 
            We see that its current digit (the least significant digit) is <b>4</b>.
            The value for four in the occurances array is <b>3</b>, 
            so we put <b>2134</b> in the third position in the array, making it <b>[-, -, 2134]</b>.
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Then, we reduce the value of that digit in the occurances array by <b>1</b>, 
            making it <b>[1, 2]</b>.
        </p>

    </li>

    <li>
        We repeat the process for <b>4321</b>, which results in <b>[4321, -, 2134]</b> and <b>[1, 1]</b>,
        and <b>1234</b>, which results in <b>[4321, 1234, 2134]</b> and <b>[0, 1]</b>.
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            Finally, we set the array to the new array we just created, 
            and we repeat the process again for each digit.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>

<p>
    LSD Radix Sort loops through the array based on the maximum number of digits in the array,
    or the number of digits in the largest number. 
    In addition, it spends additional time each pass depending on the base system it is using 
    (e.g. Binary, Decimal, Hexadecimal), with higher bases being more costly.
    This gives LSD Radix Sort a Time Complexity of <b>(n + b) &#215; d</b>, 
    where <b>n</b> is the size of the array, <b>b</b> is the base system, 
    and <b>d</b> is the maximum number of digits.
    <br>
    <br>
    Whether it is split across multiple buckets or stored in a separate array,
    LSD Radix Sort has to store a copy of the main array. 
    In addition, it has to create extra data, either the buckets themselves or the occurances array.
    The memory that these take up is based on the base system that is being used.
    These give Radix Sort a Space Complexity of <b>n + d</b>.
</p>

<h2>MSD Radix Sort</h2>
<p>
    Most Significant Digit (MSD) Radix Sort is a version of Radix Sort
    that sorts arrays starting from the most significant digit
    down to the least significant digit.
    <br>
    MSD Radix Sort begins by sorting the array based on its most significant digit
    using the <b>Buckets</b> or <b>Counting</b> methods described in LSD Radix Sort.
    <br>
    <br>
    This effectively splits the array into multiple sections.
    The number of sections depends on the base system being used. 
    Base ten, or decimal, would split the array into ten sections, 
    while hexidecmial would split it into sixteen sections.
    <br>
    <br>
    MSD Radix Sort then sorts the first section by the next most significant digit.
    Like before, this splits the section into smaller sub-sections.
    MSD Radix Sort repeats this process for all sections and subsections, 
    skipping sections if they are empty or when the least significant digit has been sorted.
    <br>
    <br>
    There are variations of both LSD and MSD Radix Sort that give them varying 
    time and space complexities for different workloads.
    This visualization's versions of both algorithms have the same time and space complexities.
</p>

